{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nm=json.load(open('../audit-spec/v3.json'))\nfor b in m['behaviors']:\n if b['id']=='B-propose':b['unknowns']=[]\n if b['id']=='B-apply':\n  b['important_branches'].append('Synchronous acceptReady stages a MsgStorageApplyResp in stepsOnAdvance, and Advance delivers it even when the public Node caller uses its permission to defer actual application. In asynchronous mode acceptReady does not stage that response: the local apply worker must process the entries and deliver their carried response after completion, preserving same-worker order. The apply response itself attests caller completion; raft does not independently inspect the state machine.')\n  b['existing_protections'].append('For a contract-compliant asynchronous worker, delaying configuration application also delays its apply response, so that work alone cannot advance reported applied past the pending configuration. Actual ApplyConfChange must precede completion of that committed configuration entry. Application writes need not be durable; completion and durability remain separate.')\n  b['source_ids']=sorted(set(b['source_ids']+['async-contract','doc-early-advance','node-early-advance','advance','leader-admit','conf-contract']))\no=m['core_overview'];o['connection']['explanation']+=' The asynchronous application boundary differs materially: the worker must finish ordered committed-entry processing before returning MsgStorageApplyResp, whereas synchronous Advance delivers the staged response under an interface permitting early application acknowledgement. Under the asynchronous caller contract, merely delaying configuration application does not clear the pending-configuration guard; this is a specific protection against that boundary mismatch, not a general membership-safety conclusion.'\no['connection']['source_ids']=sorted(set(o['connection']['source_ids']+['async-contract','apply-accept','storage-tail','leader-admit','conf-contract']))\no['open_details']=[x for x in o['open_details'] if not x.startswith('The safety effect of consecutive')]\njson.dump(m,open('core-map-v3-refined.json','w'),indent=2)\npreserve='No Fact identity, meaning, producer/consumer edge, accepted requirement, applicability scope or observation encoding changes. Existing synchronous findings and asynchronous storage-support result retain their historical bases. The added text distinguishes already documented application interfaces; it does not transfer one mode\\\\'s conclusion to another.'\nchanges={k:dict(impact='clarification',source_ids=refs,rationale=why,preserves=preserve) for k,refs,why in [('B-propose',['leader-admit','node-early-advance','doc-early-advance'],'Remove the local consecutive-admission question answered by the retained formal same-history check; execution details remain in Evidence and feedback.'),('B-apply',['async-contract','apply-accept','storage-tail','advance','conf-contract'],'Clarify the two existing completion-report producers and the asynchronous caller obligation without changing the reported-progress Fact.'),('core_overview',['async-contract','apply-accept','storage-tail','leader-admit','node-early-advance'],'Explain the important mode-dependent connection between application reporting and configuration admission; remove the answered local discriminator from open details.') ]}\ns=dict(action='research',rationale='Retain the application-boundary distinction and close the answered shared semantic unknown without generalizing the synchronous witness to asynchronous application.',map_path='core-map-v3-refined.json',map_changes=changes,sources=[],feedback=dict(ref_ids=['d4e6e11753e24e4ebd4c818214d9a268','2824d1bcdad54a3bb6ff912b2b6de50c','async-contract','storage-tail','apply-accept','advance','leader-admit','roles'],answered='Accepted review leaves no pending execution debt for the committed-entry witness. Source comparison now distinguishes the two application-completion producers: synchronous Advance delivers staged apply responses despite permitted delayed application, while asynchronous workers owe ordered processing before delivering carried apply responses. Thus a contract-compliant asynchronous worker cannot reproduce this specific reported-applied/configuration gap merely by delaying the same configuration batch. This is a sourced protection, not an executed asynchronous agreement result. Shared mapping retains the relationship and removes the answered local admission unknown.',remaining=['Next examine leadership replacement: becomeLeader resets pendingConfIndex to the existing last index, while campaign eligibility checks committed unapplied configurations. Determine which delayed-application histories these distinct guards exclude and which remain possible under the two caller contracts.'],understanding='updated',rationale='Authority replacement is another actual consumer context of reported application progress. Its conservative pending-index initialization may protect against a wider inherited tail than the steady-leader guard; source comparison can bound that protection without another fabricated scenario.'))\njson.dump(s,open('submission.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-02_10-41-34-etcd_raft-real-run --submission submission.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
